Run 2 — Disconnection environment diagnostics v1.0.2

Place the ZIP in ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop, extract it, and run:
  bash RUN_ENVIRONMENT_DIAGNOSTICS.command --workers 10

Defaults:
  Project: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness
  Python:  /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/venv/bin/python
  Reports/logs: ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop
  Python 3.10+, NumPy, PyArrow and DuckDB. SciPy is not required.

Expected runtime (unbenchmarked laptop estimates):
  Synthetic launcher tests: 20–120 seconds.
  Status-coverage report: 1–3 minutes.
  First role/bin report: approximately 5–20 minutes.
  Full first run: roughly 1–3 hours, including the local rate-count scan.
  Reused reruns are faster. SSD contention can add time.

The default continues through the entire analysis without asking questions.
--quick-only saves the first report and stops before the history-count scan.
Rerun without --quick-only to continue from exactly the same checkpoints.
--workers is a ceiling: RAM caps scan workers, and large FE models are scheduled
one at a time when required. Do not force more workers than memory permits.
--memory-gb controls the total job budget, not RAM per worker.
The downloader can keep running; both jobs share the SSD bandwidth.

What this answers
1. Which recorded timeouts correspond to the recipient disconnecting, the
   opponent disconnecting, or an unclassified role?
2. Is the entry association spread across the score distribution or concentrated
   above the already frozen ordinary-game 95th-percentile cutoff? The report
   separates row shares, score variation and exact slope contributions.
3. How do entry, seven recipient balance measures, first stage, reduced form,
   2SLS and the ordinary-game comparison change after opponent-history controls?
4. Does the score also predict recorded outoftime endings? Status is never
   invented for legacy rows without that field.

Controls, in a fixed ladder on identical rows:
  XC                    Previous rich + competitive-history controls
  XC_volume             XC + log opponent games in the past window
  XC_volume_own         XC_volume + opponent's observed disconnect rate
  XC_volume_chooser     XC_volume + opponent's observed chooser rate
  XC_both_rates         XC_volume + both rates
Both instrument definitions are evaluated separately on those same rows.
A separate full-support baseline shows what restricting rate support changed.
The primary environment-entry comparison uses the current 25% sample; its
first-10% and added-10–25% bands are reported separately, sharing accounts.

Exact history definition
The window covers game starts in the 90 days ending seven days before the focal
month begins. Count an active resolution only when it also finished strictly
before the cutoff. Use the authenticated canonical rated-game chronology with
valid two-player IDs. Exclude games involving either author and subtract the
current pair from numerator AND denominator. Do not alter the chronology itself.

The numerator comes from the certified main opportunity panel, across states.
These are observed qualifying active resolutions per rated game played. They
are NOT rates of all physical connection drops. The code checks that every
relevant numerator event is found in the canonical game universe with matching
IDs and start time. Incomplete history windows and zero other-opponent game
counts are explicitly unsupported, not zero rates. The full and common-support
sample counts are in accounting.csv and rate_coverage.json.

Receipt cohort and authors
This uses the original frozen 2,682,330-row receipt cohort. It reuses the complete
score/history/future-choice indexes, reproduces original score/history/outcome
features, then removes __PRIVATE_VALUE_1__ and __PRIVATE_VALUE_2__ from focal games, behavioral histories,
the cross-fit reference and later choices. Original spacing and factual activity
stay fixed. This package does not extend the receipt exposure cohort. Its
before/after IV fits therefore compare the same reconstructed clean cohort.

Required completed local parents
  Entry config: af3f7f1e1f6f72291a4b0a4dc709d5702629b1e87ce171376fef157c5cae4621
  25% config: 79bd3599a9956aab2314a9216926dd7d585decd30f51720f5038d79648136461
The exact reviewed summaries, producer hashes, files and schemas are checked.
The original two focal metadata quarantines are preserved. No new exclusions
for metadata errors or replacement sampling are performed.

Performance
- Project only required Parquet columns; prune chronology files and row groups
  outside the complete rate windows, and retain only required accounts.
- Count games and matched active resolutions in the same local scan. No
  all-history chronology sorting, first-pair reconstruction or Stockfish.
- Save immutable per-file count checkpoints, prepared model tables, and models.
- Reuse existing phase-independent score and future-choice feature caches.
- Spawn memory-capped scan workers; cap BLAS threads to avoid oversubscription.
- Show measured stage durations and approximate scan-stage ETAs. A whole-job
  ETA is not claimed before its heterogeneous stages have been benchmarked.
- No API calls. Do not pause or modify the continuous downloader.

Reading the results
The report ZIP is rewritten after each completed fit and each major stage. Read
READ_RESULTS.txt, estimates.csv, bins.csv, balance.csv, support.csv and
accounting.csv. All shared report files are aggregates; private row tables stay
on the research drive. Return the final RUN2_ENVIRONMENT_DIAGNOSTICS_REPORT_*.zip.

A smaller entry/balance association with stable first stage and reduced form
would support the observed-environment explanation. It cannot prove exclusion.
Check effect sizes and intervals, not just whether a p-value crosses .05. A
stable 2SLS ratio can conceal changes in both the first stage and reduced form.
Included opponent controls are not independent balance tests. The seven
recipient traits remain separate outcomes. Country is not estimated: this
package has no authenticated historical profile source and does not call APIs.

Inference keeps the original shared-account OR exposure-date CR0 covariance,
fixed score units and AR intervals. Nonpositive covariance is flagged without
repair. These are exploratory nominal intervals after prior results; they are
not sequentially valid tests or an automatic abstract/title approval rule.

Fix in v1.0.1
A legacy all-null status field can be read as floating NaN after DuckDB converts
the Parquet null type to nullable integer. Version 1.0.0 checked only for Python
None and wrongly treated NaN as a recorded non-timeout status. Version 1.0.1
recognizes both missing representations, retains the verified active_timeout
labels and every entry row, and runs optional ending regressions only on cohorts
with fully recorded endings. It also checks known statuses in mixed cohorts;
genuine disagreements and missing expansion metadata still stop estimation.

Before fitting models, status_coverage.json reports missingness and any conflicts
for every cohort. The exact completed v1.0.0/v1.0.1 prepared Parquets are authenticated
and adopted into the new run. Old files/receipts are untouched. Diagnostic models
are refitted under the corrected validation; compatible upstream feature caches
remain reusable. No source data, entry labels, author rules or specifications
are altered by this repair.

Fix in v1.0.2
The v1.0.1 production log shows finite input becoming nonfinite during fixed-
effect absorption, before the small continuous-control regression. The exact
trigger on the private Mac data has not been reproduced here. A focused test
does reproduce the old solver's error-handling defect: NaN group means can
produce a false convergence decision and then reach the eigenvalue routine.

The environment models now use a separate checked implementation of the same
alternating fixed-effect projection: contiguous columns, sorted group sums,
exact power-of-two scaling, finite checks, non-increasing sums of squares, and
a final check of every group's residual mean. It retains the original tolerance
and iteration limit. No ridge, row deletion or covariance repair is added.
The authenticated vendored packages are unchanged. The same certified entry
coefficients, standard errors, sample counts and row digests must reproduce.

Numerics are checked against direct dummy-variable least squares and the older
independent group accumulator, plus a large synthetic stress run. The optional
tests/stress_numerics.py documents its runtime and memory estimate; it is not
part of the normal quick launcher tests. evidence/numerical_stress_local.json
records the actual local stress result, not a Mac production benchmark.

Prepared Parquets are adopted byte-for-byte from the exact v1.0.1 or v1.0.0
configuration. Because the numerical engine changed, all diagnostic models are
refitted. Old successful model receipts remain untouched. Reports now include
numerics.json and numerical_progress.json, and refresh after each committed fit.
If one fit fails, already running fits finish, no further fits launch, and the
aggregate report preserves completed estimates and the failure diagnostic.
The remaining full analysis still needs to run against your private Mac data.
